<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
The Iron web framework builds a response from a tuple of modifiers, for example
<code>Response::with((status::Ok, body))</code>. A body that is added this way is sent without a
<code>Content-Type</code> header unless one of the modifiers sets it, and browsers then guess the
content type from the body. If the body contains user-controlled data, an attacker can make the
browser render it as HTML and run a script in the context of the application.
</p>
<p>
This query finds user-controlled data that is part of a modifier tuple passed to
<code>Response::with</code>. A tuple that also contains a content type parsed from a string literal
that does not name HTML or XML, such as <code>"text/plain".parse::&lt;Mime&gt;().unwrap()</code>, is
not reported.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Set an explicit content type that is not HTML when the body is not markup, or encode the
user-controlled data for HTML before it is added to the body.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the path of the request is written into the body of the response without
a content type, so a path that contains markup is rendered as HTML:
</p>
<sample src="IronResponseXssBad.rs" />
<p>
In the following corrected example, the response declares that its body is plain text:
</p>
<sample src="IronResponseXssGood.rs" />
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<p>
A content type that is set by another kind of modifier, such as <code>Header(ContentType::json())</code>,
is not recognized, so the body of such a response is still reported. A content type is recognized
only when it is parsed directly from a string literal and flows to the tuple through local
variables, <code>unwrap</code>, <code>expect</code> or <code>?</code>. The type that the literal is
parsed into is not checked.
</p>
</section>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/">Cross Site Scripting (XSS)</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html">CWE-79</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
